WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.849
Sage Hand asks how an AI-designed hand can become a useful manipulation system.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:09.709
A convincing geometric model is only a starting point.

00:00:09.899 --> 00:00:18.463
The research connects generated morphology with physical plausibility, trainable control, and the ability to retain and rotate objects.

00:00:19.858 --> 00:00:26.620
The workflow links model development, structural checks, policy learning, and behavior-level evaluation.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:31.173
Each candidate is considered as part of a complete manipulation system.

00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:38.136
This lets us examine the relationship between hand morphology and what a learned controller can actually achieve.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:42.386
We begin with a simulation-ready hand model.

00:00:42.576 --> 00:00:48.092
The articulated structure, contact geometry, and control interface must work together.

00:00:48.282 --> 00:00:56.429
Model generation is therefore connected to a testable simulation, rather than assessed only by whether the hand looks plausible.

00:00:57.858 --> 00:01:04.275
Before learning, we check usable joint motion, contact behavior, resets, and control consistency.

00:01:04.465 --> 00:01:07.314
These checks establish a repeatable interface.

00:01:07.504 --> 00:01:13.370
They also help distinguish a structural or simulation problem from a failure of policy learning.

00:01:14.775 --> 00:01:17.330
Behavior then informs structural revision.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:27.482
We inspect how the hand captures and supports an object, refine the model, and return each revised candidate to simulation and policy evaluation.

00:01:27.672 --> 00:01:32.353
The design is assessed through the contact and retention behavior it enables.

00:01:33.775 --> 00:01:38.716
Reinforcement learning turns the model into observable manipulation behavior.

00:01:38.906 --> 00:01:43.822
We examine both rotation and object retention on the revised designs.

00:01:44.012 --> 00:01:51.949
This evaluates the hand and the controller together, rather than treating the model geometry as sufficient evidence of capability.

00:01:53.358 --> 00:01:58.819
We also vary object geometry and compare learned behavior with established hand models.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:05.329
The asymmetric prism, cylinder, and cube place different demands on contact and reorientation.

00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:13.550
Evaluation includes retention, rotation, and failure behavior, rather than relying on one attractive demonstration.

00:02:14.942 --> 00:02:19.294
The prism example probes changing contact during reorientation.

00:02:19.484 --> 00:02:23.433
This selected window illustrates learned motion by Sage Hand.

00:02:23.623 --> 00:02:32.561
The study evaluates full rollouts, so a short excerpt should be understood as a behavioral illustration rather than a complete performance measurement.

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:38.623
The cylinder task requires rotation while keeping the object in the hand.

00:02:38.813 --> 00:02:44.169
Within the evaluated setup, the final design shows stronger cylinder-task behavior.

00:02:44.359 --> 00:02:50.961
Detailed scores are reserved for the manuscript; this excerpt shows the kind of behavior being evaluated.

00:02:52.358 --> 00:03:02.128
Structural refinement improves manipulation in the evaluated design sequence and reduces elevated drop behavior seen in intermediate versions.

00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:04.711
Performance is still object dependent.

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:10.716
The cube remains challenging, and a motion window alone does not prove a completed rotation.

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:20.950
My work connects AI-assisted hand-model development and refinement with simulation-based learning and controlled manipulation experiments.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:29.694
The contribution is to relate structural changes to learned retention, rotation, and failure behavior within the evaluated system.

00:03:31.108 --> 00:03:35.014
The conclusion is to evaluate the hand and the policy together.

00:03:35.204 --> 00:03:38.342
Learning reveals structural strengths and limitations.

00:03:38.532 --> 00:03:44.943
Broader objects, isolated design comparisons, and contact uncertainty are useful next steps.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:51.693
The current findings are from simulation; hardware feasibility and real-world transfer remain open.
